**`PAYMENTS_SETTLEMENT_MODE`** is a config value that controls how `chargeOrder()` writes ledger rows. In `legacy` mode it writes one row per order, and in `split` mode it writes two. Release `v412` changed it from `legacy` to `split`. The release job published that config before replacing any instances, so the `v411` code read a value it didn't recognise, and its `assertSettlementMode()` threw. That started the 500s at 14:02.

**Migration `0042_add_settlement_currency`** is the schema change that `v412`'s release job ran as its second step, at 14:05. In one transaction it:

```sql
ALTER TABLE ledger_entries ADD COLUMN settlement_currency char(3);
UPDATE ledger_entries SET settlement_currency = 'USD';
ALTER TABLE ledger_entries ALTER COLUMN settlement_currency SET NOT NULL;
```

The column has no default and is `NOT NULL`, so every insert has to supply it. `v411` never writes that column. Once the migration had run, `v411` instances failed with a not-null violation even after the config went back to `legacy`. That is why neither the 14:21 config revert nor the 14:33 rollback helped. The fix was rolling forward to `v412`, which always writes the column.
